<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kiribati</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kiribati is an island republic in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, made up of 33 atolls and reef islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a>, Phoenix and Line groups, with its capital at South Tarawa. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a> became a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1892 and later part of the Gilbert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellice-islands">Ellice Islands</a> colony, and Tarawa saw a fierce battle in 1943. It became independent in 1979, its name being the local form of 'Gilberts'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kiribati.jpg" alt="Kiribati" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hanzlan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kiribati.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kiribati has a total land area of 811 km2 spread over 3,441,810 km2 of ocean. Its islands, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banaba">Banaba</a> in the west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiritimati">Kiritimati</a> in the east, straddle the equator and the 180th meridian. They fall into three groups: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a>, sixteen atolls about 1,500 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenix-islands">Phoenix Islands</a>, eight atolls and coral islands; and the Line Islands, eight atolls and one reef. Banaba is a raised coral island that was once a rich source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phosphate">phosphates</a>. The rest of the land is sand and reef rock islets that rise only one or two metres above sea level, with thin, calcareous soil. Kiritimati has the largest land area of any atoll in the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited by Austronesian peoples speaking the same Oceanic language since sometime between 3000 BCE and 1300 CE. Oral historians suggest that seafarers from Melanesia came first, later visited by Austronesian seafarers from the west. Around 1300 CE a mass departure from Samoa added Polynesian ancestry and culture. From about the 15th century the northern islands came under chiefly rule (uea), while the central and southern islands were ruled by councils of elders (unimwaane); Tabiteuea alone is known to have kept an egalitarian society. Clans and chiefs fought over land, and some islands used guns and cannon-equipped ships supplied by Europeans to destroy their foes.</p><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-islands">Gilbert Islands</a> was given around 1820 by the Russian admiral Adam von Krusenstern and the French captain Louis Duperrey, after the British captain Thomas Gilbert, who sighted some of the islands in 1788. The British annexed the Gilberts as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1892. From 1916 the Gilbert and Ellice Islands Colony took its name from them, and it lasted until the Ellice Islands became Tuvalu in 1976. Kiribati became independent from the United Kingdom in 1979, with the Gilberts, the Phoenix and Line islands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banaba">Banaba</a>. The name is the Gilbertese rendering of Gilberts. Phosphate mining on Banaba was exhausted by independence, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banabans">Banabans</a> mostly live on Rabi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kiribati is a parliamentary democratic republic under the constitution promulgated on 12 July 1979. Its capital, Tarawa, is made up of islets joined by causeways, and more than half the population lives there. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of November 2020 counted 119,940 people. The I-Kiribati are a Micronesian people who speak Gilbertese, an Oceanic language; English is the other official language. The country joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1999.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kiribati” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tuvalu became independent in 1978 and the Gilberts, as <b>Kiribati</b>, in 1979.</blockquote><a href="/state/gilbert-and-ellice-islands">Gilbert and Ellice Islands</a></li><li><blockquote>The atoll is now part of <b>Kiribati</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-abemama">Kingdom of Abemama</a></li></ul></section>
